<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's National Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as PNP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> is one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>'s two main political parties, founded in 1938 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-manley">Norman Manley</a> amid labour unrest. Social democratic in outlook, it governed under Manley in the years before independence and has alternated in power with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica-labour-party">Jamaica Labour Party</a> since 1962, under leaders including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-manley">Michael Manley</a> and P. J. Patterson.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peoples-national-party.jpg" alt="People's National Party" width="320" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: People's National Party of Jamaica · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:People%27s_National_Party_(Jamaica)_Logo_(2021_-_present).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Norman Washington Manley led the party from its founding in 1938 until his death in 1969. It is the second-oldest party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey">Marcus Garvey</a>'s People's Political Party of 1929. It won only four of 32 seats at the first universal election in 1944, and in 1949 took more votes than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica-labour-party">Jamaica Labour Party</a> but fewer seats, 13 to 17. In 1954 it expelled the Marxist Richard Hart and three others, known as the four Hs, which parted it from the Trade Union Congress.</p><p>The party first won in 1955, with 18 of 32 seats. Manley became chief minister and in 1959 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>'s first premier, and his governments opened secondary education to many poorer Jamaicans through state scholarships. After Jamaica voted in 1961 to leave the West Indies Federation, Manley called an election for April 1962, which the JLP won by 26 seats to 19.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-manley">Norman Manley</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-manley">Michael Manley</a> the party returned in 1972 committed to democratic socialism and closer ties with the Global South, and won a landslide in 1976. After years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and rising unemployment it was routed in 1980 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-seaga">Edward Seaga</a>'s JLP in a campaign marked by violence, boycotted the snap election of 1983 and returned in 1989. P. J. Patterson, leader from 1992, became the first Jamaican leader to win three successive general elections, in 1993, 1997 and 2002.</p><p>Portia Simpson-Miller succeeded him in 2006 as the party's first female president and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>'s first female prime minister. The PNP lost in 2007, ending 18 years in power, won 42 of 63 seats in 2011 and lost by one seat in 2016. After a heavy defeat in September 2020, Mark Golding was elected its sixth president in November 2020.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party describes itself as social democratic, and its constitution calls it democratic socialist; it also adheres to republicanism. It is a full member of the Socialist International and a member of COPPPAL, and from 1957 to 1962 it belonged to the West Indies Federal Labour Party in the parliament of the West Indies Federation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “People's National Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Politics has been a contest between the Jamaica Labour Party and the <b>People's National Party</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a></li></ul></section>
